## 4. Capture and shots (09.1)

### The capture pipeline (`src/web/capture.ts`)

- **A capture mounts a hidden canvas** at the image's size (off screen, with the view's pixel ratio so lines, notes and arrowheads, which are sized in screen pixels, look as they do on screen). It holds the lighting and the shapes with no highlights, uses the capture's camera as its own, draws one frame and reads it back as a PNG, then unmounts, freeing its WebGL context. The view is never touched. Captures run one at a time.
  - **Why not an offscreen render target of the view's renderer:** three.js draws render targets in linear color without tone mapping, so the colors wouldn't match the screen. A canvas of its own has the same settings as the view.
  - Labels and a legend (§6) will go on a 2D canvas over the image.
- **It waits for the holes:** if the hole cutting (`csg.ts`) is still running, the capture waits for it, so a capture never shows uncut walls.
- **One pipeline for everything:** the editor's shutter, Walk shots, and every `render_view`.
- **Hidden nodes stay hidden** (as Orlando set them). Isolation is ignored: a capture shows the whole document.

### The shutter

- **`K`** (a free key), or the **camera button** on the view bar: captures the view exactly as framed, at the canvas size × the device pixel ratio, capped at 4096 px on the long edge. It's a clean capture with notes and lines shown. Next to it, the **shots button** (with the count of shots) shows or hides the Shots panel.
- **A short white flash** over the view, and the new thumbnail slides into the Shots panel. The panel opens on the first shot of a session.

### The Shots panel (`src/web/ShotsPanel.tsx`)

- **Floating like the inspector and the Entities panel** (`useFloating`): bottom right by default, collapsible, with a max height. It opens from the view bar's shots button, and by itself on this tab's first shot.
- **A grid of thumbnails,** newest first, each with its ID, caption, and an agent badge for the agent's shots. Hovering shows the caption and when it was taken, and marks it "the level changed since" when it has.
- **Click a shot to go to it:**
  - an editor shot flies the camera to its view (the 09 flight, which already exists);
  - a walk shot enters Walk mode at its pose, paused, with the shot's settings (for this walk only, without changing the player camera).
- **Each shot has:** Caption (inline edit), Copy image (the clipboard, as PNG), Save (download `shot_3.png`) and Delete (with a confirm).
- **The panel has Save all:** one zip (`<scene>-shots.zip`, with `fflate`, small and dependency-free), images named `shot_3 caption.png`.
- **Selecting a shot** doesn't change the scene's selection. The agent reads shots with `get_shots` (§6) and doesn't need a selection to know which one Orlando means, because Orlando names it.

### Protocol

```
client → server  { type: "add_shot", camera, width, height, caption?, image: base64 PNG }
client → server  { type: "update_shot", id, caption } / { type: "remove_shot", id }
server → client  { type: "shots", shots: [ShotRecord] }       // the open document's, on open and every change
```

The image goes over the WebSocket once (a few MB at most, locally) so there's one code path, and it's read back over HTTP.

